course: dictionary
lesson: 14102
title: Merchandise: goods to buy and sell
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Merchandise» means goods that are bought and sold, especially in shops. Look at the words around it to work out its meaning. «The football club sells official merchandise, like scarves and shirts.» Scarves and shirts are things you can buy, so merchandise must mean goods for sale.
explain: Fans often call it «merch», a short, informal word for merchandise. «Lily bought band merch: a mug and a poster with her favourite singer on them.» The mug and poster have the band's picture on them, so merch means items sold with a band, film, team or game's name or picture.
explain: «Merchandise» can also be a verb, which means to show goods in a way that helps them sell. «The shop moved the sweets to the front to merchandise them.» Putting the sweets at the front is a way of arranging them so people buy more. Ask yourself: what would fit in this sentence? Then try your meaning and check it still makes sense.
fact: «Merchandise» means goods that are bought and sold, especially in shops.
fact: «Merch» is an informal short form of merchandise, often used for things sold with a band, film, team or game on them.
fact: Merchandise can be a noun (the goods) or a verb (to promote and sell goods, often by how they are displayed).
fact: Phrases you may see: «official merchandise», «band merch», «merchandise stall».
fact: In maths, merchandise appears in money problems about prices, discounts and profit.
exercise: «The football club sells official merchandise, like scarves and shirts.» What does merchandise mean here? | Players who play in the team | *Goods that are bought and sold, like scarves and shirts | Tickets for a match | The name of the stadium
exercise: Mia says her favourite band's «merch» is what she wants for her birthday. Which of these is most likely merch? | Pencils from a school | A bus ticket | *A T-shirt with the band's logo on it | A library book
exercise: «The shop moved the sweets to the front to merchandise them.» What does merchandise mean in this sentence? | To pack goods into boxes for a long trip | To throw goods away | *To display and sell goods so people buy more | To make goods out of wood
test: «Lily bought band merch: a mug and a poster.» What is merch in this sentence? | *Things with a band's name or picture on, sold to fans | Rules for a game | Food from a restaurant | Lessons at school
test: Which is the informal short form of merchandise? | *Merch | Merry | Mercy | Merchant
exam: «The shop merchandised its sweets at the front, so more children saw them.» What does merchandised mean here? | Eaten quickly | Given away free to everyone | Hidden at the back of the shop | *Displayed and sold to get people to buy
